{"id": "cc510542-db21-408e-98cb-97a3be442608", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Found it — the repo is actually named **`nebula-payments-api`** (not `payments-api`), and heads up: the current policy keeps the newest **3** images, not 10.

More importantly, the answer to your question is **no, `prod-*` and `release-*` tags are not protected**:

```json
{
  "rulePriority": 1,
  "description": "Keep only last 3 images",
  "selection": {
    "tagStatus": "any",
    "countType": "imageCountMoreThan",
    "countNumber": 3
  },
  "action": { "type": "expire" }
}
```

There's only one rule, and it uses `tagStatus: "any"` — meaning it applies to **every image regardless of tag**, including `prod-*` and `release-*`. There's no higher-priority rule with a `tagPrefixList` carving out an exception for those tags, so once a `prod-*` or `release-*` image falls outside the 3 most recent pushes, it will be expired just like anything else.

If you want those tags protected, you'd typically add a lower-priority-number rule like:

```json
{
  "rulePriority": 1,
  "selection": {
    "tagStatus": "tagged",
    "tagPrefixList": ["prod-", "release-"],
    "countType": "imageCountMoreThan",
    "countNumber": 10
  },
  "action": { "type": "expire" }
}
```
...and bump the existing "any" rule to priority 2, so prod/release images get evaluated separately with their own retention count before the catch-all rule expires everything else.

Want me to double check the retention count — did you mean 3 (current) or did you expect 10 to already be set, suggesting someone may have changed this policy recently?", "type": "text"}], "created_at": null}

